---
import CaseStudy from "~/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseHero from "~/components/case/CaseHero.astro";
import Closing from "~/components/case/Closing.astro";
import Lessons from "~/components/case/Lessons.astro";
import NextNav from "~/components/case/NextNav.astro";
import Scenes from "~/components/case/Scenes.astro";
import SceneStep from "~/components/case/SceneStep.astro";
import StepShot from "~/components/case/StepShot.astro";
import StepShots from "~/components/case/StepShots.astro";
import Section from "~/components/ui/Section.astro";
import lobby from "~/assets/shots/r99-lobby.webp";
import combat from "~/assets/shots/r99-game-combat.webp";
import library from "~/assets/shots/tutor-library.webp";
import scryfall from "~/assets/shots/scryfall-home.webp";
import scryfallPhone from "~/assets/shots/scryfall-home-mobile.webp";
---

<CaseStudy
  title="MTG suite · Guus Ekkelenkamp"
  og="mtg-suite"
  description="Case study: the Magic: The Gathering tools I build for my friends, from a Commander table in the browser to a shared deck library."
  lightbox
>
  <CaseHero
    project="mtg-suite"
    meta="Personal · 2026 · two of the tools are open source"
    title="MTG suite"
    line="The Magic tools I build for my friends, so we can learn the game together and play it from our own sofas."
  />
  <Scenes id="story" title="The story behind the tools" gallery>
    <SceneStep scene="itch">
      <p>
        Magic: The Gathering is the card game my friends and I play most, usually Commander: four players,
        each with a 100-card deck built around one legendary creature, the commander. It’s a brilliant game
        with over 33,000 cards, and plenty of them bend a rule somewhere. We wanted an easier way to learn it,
        and a way to play on the evenings we can’t sit at one table.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fun part is the audience. My testers are friends, and they tell me exactly what they think, which
        is how we do it in Rotterdam anyway. A fix can go out in the afternoon and get played that evening.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="table">
      <h3>A table in the browser</h3>
      <p>
        Rules of 99 is Commander in the browser for two to four players, joined with a six-character room
        code. I didn’t write a rules engine, that’s a lifetime’s work. An open-source one already knows the
        rules and runs headless on my server. My part is carrying each question the engine asks to the right
        browser, and each answer back.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Magic a spell doesn’t happen right away. It goes on the stack, a pile where the last thing played
        resolves first, and every player gets priority, a chance to respond. At four players that’s a lot of
        questions, so I trimmed the stops and added auto-resolve for moments when you have nothing to play. It
        never skips a spell aimed at you or your cards.
      </p>
      <StepShot
        src={lobby}
        alt="Rules of 99 home screen after signing in"
        caption="Home after signing in: your decks and open tables."
      />
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="hands">
      <h3>Four players, one game</h3>
      <p>
        The server holds the whole game and sends each player only their own view. The others only see how
        many cards are in your hand, and libraries never leave the server. You can reload and sit back down,
        and finished games can be replayed turn by turn.
      </p>
      <p>
        Targeting taught me to trust nothing. The engine numbers players from 0 and cards from 1, and the
        server looked up card ids first, so picking player 1 quietly picked card 1. Every option now says what
        kind of thing it is.
      </p>
      <StepShot
        src={combat}
        alt="Rules of 99 during combat: four attackers, with arrows to the player they attack"
        caption="Combat: four attackers, each with an arrow to its target."
      />
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="decks">
      <h3>Where our decks live</h3>
      <p>
        Tutor is our deck library and builder, and it’s strict about the game. A card only fits a deck when
        its colours sit inside the commander’s, and banned cards stay out, so the search is exact. Each deck
        shows its curve and power bracket, so we can pick decks that suit each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        Friends can share their paper collections with the group, so before anyone buys a card you can see who
        already has one in a binder. When a deck is ready, one click takes it into Rules of 99, which can only
        read your decks.
      </p>
      <StepShot
        src={library}
        alt="Tutor deck library with commander art for each deck"
        caption="The library: every deck at a glance."
      />
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="search">
      <h3>Finding cards in plain words</h3>
      <p>
        Scryfall is the card search most players use, and its syntax takes practice. In mine you describe the
        cards in normal words. A model writes the query, the server tests it against Scryfall first, and you
        get a stricter and a broader reading, each with a breakdown of what every part does. You get your
        cards and learn the syntax on the way. For deck building there’s also a set of open-source Claude Code
        skills, one more tool for the group.
      </p>
      <StepShots>
        <StepShot
          src={scryfall}
          alt="Scryfall in plain words: a search box with a plain-language request and suggestion chips"
          caption="Describe the cards in normal words; the query is written and checked for you."
        />
        <StepShot src={scryfallPhone} alt="Scryfall in plain words on a phone" caption="On a phone." phone />
      </StepShots>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="friends">
      <h3>Building for people you know</h3>
      <p>
        My friends can file a bug report straight from the table. One press turns it into a GitHub issue, and
        closing the issue marks the report fixed. Every merge to main lands on my homelab, so the next game
        has the fix.
      </p>
      <p>
        New cards arrive overnight. A nightly job pulls in the engine’s latest card data, checks that the
        engine still starts on it, and opens a pull request. The engine itself only moves when I move it.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
  </Scenes>
  <Section id="lessons" title="Lessons">
    <Lessons
      items={[
        {
          title: "Test against the real thing.",
          text: "A mock server once hid a bug that made the whole hand unclickable in real games, so I deleted it. Every test now plays against the real server.",
        },
        {
          title: "Faster isn’t always better.",
          text: "When I removed one more stop to speed games up, the scripted test players interacted less, and one seat died early. Fewer questions meant less Magic, so that stop came back.",
        },
        {
          title: "Keep the rules honest, even for friends.",
          text: "Turning off the free first mulligan would have meant patching the engine to break a rule, so I left that toggle out.",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Section>
  <Closing
    line="With my friends I’m usually the one who plans the evening and chases the group chat for a date. These days I bring the table too, and I get the bug reports."
    stack="Built with: Java, React, TypeScript, WebSockets, Node and SQLite."
  />
  <NextNav
    prev={{ href: "/projects/homelab/", small: "Previous project", label: "&larr; Homelab" }}
    next={{ href: "/projects/", small: "Back to", label: "all projects" }}
  />
</CaseStudy>
